= Decentralized finance =

== Overview ==
'''Decentralized finance''' is a family of financial applications and protocols that use distributed ledgers and smart contracts to provide functions such as trading, lending, borrowing, asset management, and settlement.

Many DeFi systems are designed for open, composable interaction between smart contracts and ledger accounts. Common components include tokens, lending protocols, decentralized exchanges, automated market makers, collateral, stable-value assets, and oracles.

'Decentralized' is a degree and architecture claim, not a guarantee. Governance keys, developer teams, validators, front ends, oracle providers, custodians, stablecoin issuers, and concentrated voting or liquidity can create central points of influence.

DeFi does not mean risk-free or intermediary-free in every sense. Smart-contract bugs, oracle failures, liquidity shocks, leverage, governance attacks, transaction ordering, and external asset dependencies can create substantial risks.

== Boundaries ==
* DeFi ≠ complete absence of intermediaries or governance
* DeFi ≠ decentralized exchange only
* on-chain automation ≠ elimination of financial risk

